    "https://werkenbij.vu.nl/vacatures/phd-vacancy-knowledge-creation-and-sharing-in-augmented-organisations-amsterdam-1340642": {
      "title": "PhD Vacancy: Knowledge Creation and Sharing in Augmented Organisations",
      "description": "PhD Vacancy: Knowledge Creation and Sharing in Augmented Organisations | Werken bij VU Vacatures Faculteit/Dienst Faculteiten Academisch Centrum Tandheelkunde Amsterdam (ACTA) Faculteit der Bètawetenschappen Faculteit der Gedrags- en Bewegingswetenschappen Faculteit der  Sociale en Geesteswetenschappen Faculteit der Geneeskunde Faculteit der Rechtsgeleerdheid School of Business and Economics Diensten Bestuurszaken Communicatie & Marketing Dienst Universitaire Relaties & Fondswerving Facilitaire Campus Organisatie Financiën HRM, Arbo & Milieu Informatietechnologie Student- en Onderwijszaken Universiteitsbibliotheek Jong talent PhD Student-assistent Stage Over ons Over ons VU arbeidsvoorwaarden: uitgebreid en flexibel VU persoonlijk: ontwikkelen, erkennen en waarderen VU identiteit: veilig, verantwoord en inclusief VU Campus: groen, rookvrij en sportief VU sollicitatie: proces, screening en participatie Ik ben een kandidaat Ga naar mijn persoonlijke pagina Log in als medewerker Log in 0 NL EN PhD Vacancy: Knowledge Creation and Sharing in Augmented Organisations Ga terug VU Hoofdgebouw 1 / 1 MSc Publicatiedatum 22-09-2026 Amsterdam School of Business and Economics Sluit op 18-10-2026 Solliciteer direct PhD Vacancy: Knowledge Creation and Sharing in Augmented Organisations VU Hoofdgebouw Amsterdam 1 / 1 School of Business and Economics MSc Sluit op 18-10-2026 Publicatiedatum 22-09-2026 Solliciteer direct Join us as a PhD researcher exploring how generative AI is reshaping learning, expertise and workplace relationships. Help uncover how people and organizations adapt to the future of wor Your function We are looking for a doctoral researcher to study how generative AI is changing learning, expertise and relationships at work. The PhD is a four year project that is part of EMANAIRE (Empowering Human Agency in AI-Augmented Futures of Work) , a Horizon Europe Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Network. You will be based at Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam, while working as part of an international network of doctoral researchers and universities. The programme includes joint training, research seminars, methods workshops and opportunities to collaborate with partner institutions across Europe. A six-month secondment is planned as part of the project. Because this position is part of an MSCA Doctoral Network, specific eligibility conditions apply : Candidates can be of any nationality but must not already hold a doctoral degree at the time of recruitment. Under the MSCA mobility rule , candidates must not have resided or carried out their main activity (work, studies, etc.) in the Netherlands for more than 12 months during the 36 months immediately before their recruitment date. Your profile Your research will focus on what happens when generative AI becomes part of everyday organisational life. You will study how early-career employees learn their jobs, seek help, develop expertise and deal with responsibility when AI is increasingly involved in their work. This is a qualitative research project , with substantial ethnographic fieldwork where you will be interviewing and observing people in organizations. Relevant masters degree backgrounds include organisation studies, sociology, anthropology, STS, information systems or related fields. We are looking for exceptional grades in a masters programme, particularly demonstrating strong writing, reading, critical thinking, and presentation skills. We are not looking for a primarily quantitative or experimental researcher. The project does not involve building AI systems. This project requires someone who is curious about people and organisations. You need to be comfortable approaching and talking with a range of people, building relationships in the field, listening carefully and noticing what happens around you. Ethnographic research also requires persistence. You will be expected to problem solve on the go, keep detailed fieldnotes, conduct and analyse interviews, and share your findings with the team. KIN is a collaborative research group and we regularly share work-in-progress for feedback. What do we offer? A challenging position in a socially engaged organisation. At VU Amsterdam, you contribute to education, research and service for a better world. And that is valuable. So in return for your efforts, we offer you: a salary of € 3.204,00 (PhD) and maximum € 4.051,00 (PhD) gross per month in the fourth year, for a full-time employment an employment contract of initially 1 year. If there is sufficient perspective, this will be extended to a total of 4 years. The successful candidate will join an international research community, starting approximately January 2027. You will develop your doctoral project with supervision at Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam and collaboration across the EMANAIRE network. We also offer you attractive fringe benefits and arrangements. Some examples: A full-time 38-hour working week comes with a holiday leave entitlement of 232 hours per year. If you choose to work 40 hours, you have 96 extra holiday leave hours on an annual basis. For part-timers, this is calculated pro rata. 8% holiday allowance and 8.3% end-of-year bonus contribution to commuting expenses optional model for designing a personalized benefits package a wide range of sports facilities which staff may use at a modest charge About us KIN Center for Digital Innovation The KIN Center for Digital Innovation is an internationally recognized research group at the intersection of organization studies, strategy, and technology. KIN researchers study how organizations and industries transform in response to major societal and technological transitions. The center has a strong track record in qualitative and practice-based research and actively collaborates with industry partners on issues at the frontier of organizational and managerial relevance. You will join a dynamic group of researchers working on governance, strategic change, and cross-sector collaboration. School of Business and Economics We at the School of Business and Economics (SBE) at VU Amsterdam bring together socially relevant teaching and research in the areas of business administration and economics. We focus on real-life issues that have a huge impact on society, economics and ecology: from robotics to big data, and from job market participation to change management. Collaboration and cooperation, transparency and social responsibility are four keywords that embody our approach. Students, researchers and staff at SBE share real-world knowledge in order to devise solutions together to global economic and social challenges. In order to make a positive impact on the world, society and the lives of others. Are you interested in joining SBE? You will work in a stimulating, dynamic and international environment with motivated colleagues dedicated to helping society make informed choices. SBE employs roughly 600 staff, with 7,250 students enrolled in the Bachelor’s and Master’s programmes and 1,700 executive students. Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam stands for values-driven education and research. We are open-minded experts with the ability to think freely - a broader mind. Maintaining an entrepreneurial perspective and concentrating on diversity, significance and humanity, we work on sustainable solutions with social impact. By joining forces, across the boundaries of disciplines, we work towards a better world for people and planet. Together we create a safe and respectful working and study climate, and an inspiring environment for education and research. Learn more about our codes of conduct We are located on one physical campus, in the heart of Amsterdam's Zuidas business district, with excellent location and accessibility.  Over 6,150 staff work at the VU and over 31,000 students attend academic education. Diversity Diversity is the driving force of VU Amsterdam. VU wants to be accessible and receptive to diversity in disciplines, cultures, ideas, nationalities, beliefs, preferences and worldviews. We believe that trust, respect, interest and differences lead to new insights and innovation, to sharpness and clarity, to excellence and a broader understanding. We stand for an inclusive community and believe that diversity and internationalisation contribute to the quality of education, research and our services. Therefore, we are always searching for people whose backgrounds and experience contribute to the diversity of the VU community. Interested Are you interested in this position and do you believe that your experience will contribute to the further development of our university? In that case, we encourage you to submit your application. For your application, we would like to learn something about how you think and what you notice. In a one page cover letter, please introduce yourself and your interest in the specific position briefly, and then address the following assignment: Drawing on your own experience of generative AI in a workplace, university or other institution, as well as some additional reading, tell us what you think “AI anomie” might mean. Ground your answer in something you have actually experienced or observed. There is no correct answer. We are interested in how you make sense of a specific experience and how you connect to our project’s topic. In a two page CV , please share relevant experience, including your masters programme and grades. Submitting a diploma and a reference check are part of the application process. Applications received by e-mail will not be considered. Acquisition in response to this advertisement is not appreciated. Your function We are looking for a doctoral researcher to study how generative AI is changing learning, expertise and relationships at work. The PhD is a four year project that is part of EMANAIRE (Empowering Human Agency in AI-Augmented Futures of Work) , a Horizon Europe Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Network. You will be based at Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam, while working as part of an international network of doctoral researchers and universities. The programme includes joint training, research seminars, methods workshops and opportunities to collaborate with partner institutions across Europe. A six-month secondment is planned as part of the project. Because this position is part of an MSCA Doctoral Network, specific eligibility conditions apply : Candidates can be of any nationality but must not already hold a doctoral degree at the time of recruitment. Under the MSCA mobility rule , candidates must not have resided or carried out their main activity (work, studies, etc.) in the Netherlands for more than 12 months during the 36 months immediately before their recruitment date. Your profile Your research will focus on what happens when generative AI becomes part of everyday organisational life. You will study how early-career employees learn their jobs, seek help, develop expertise and deal with responsibility when AI is increasingly involved in their work. This is a qualitative research project , with substantial ethnographic fieldwork where you will be interviewing and observing people in organizations. Relevant masters degree backgrounds include organisation studies, sociology, anthropology, STS, information systems or related fields. We are looking for exceptional grades in a masters programme, particularly demonstrating strong writing, reading, critical thinking, and presentation skills. We are not looking for a primarily quantitative or experimental researcher. The project does not involve building AI systems. This project requires someone who is curious about people and organisations. You need to be comfortable approaching and talking with a range of people, building relationships in the field, listening carefully and noticing what happens around you. Ethnographic research also requires persistence. You will be expected to problem solve on the go, keep detailed fieldnotes, conduct and analyse interviews, and share your findings with the team. KIN is a collaborative research group and we regularly share work-in-progress for feedback. What do we offer? A challenging position in a socially engaged organisation. At VU Amsterdam, you contribute to education, research and service for a better world. And that is valuable. So in return for your efforts, we offer you: a salary of € 3.204,00 (PhD) and maximum € 4.051,00 (PhD) gross per month in the fourth year, for a full-time employment an employment contract of initially 1 year. If there is sufficient perspective, this will be extended to a total of 4 years. The successful candidate will join an international research community, starting approximately January 2027. You will develop your doctoral project with supervision at Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam and collaboration across the EMANAIRE network. We also offer you attractive fringe benefits and arrangements. Some examples: A full-time 38-hour working week comes with a holiday leave entitlement of 232 hours per year. If you choose to work 40 hours, you have 96 extra holiday leave hours on an annual basis. For part-timers, this is calculated pro rata. 8% holiday allowance and 8.3% end-of-year bonus contribution to commuting expenses optional model for designing a personalized benefits package a wide range of sports facilities which staff may use at a modest charge About us KIN Center for Digital Innovation The KIN Center for Digital Innovation is an internationally recognized research group at the intersection of organization studies, strategy, and technology. KIN researchers study how organizations and industries transform in response to major societal and technological transitions. The center has a strong track record in qualitative and practice-based research and actively collaborates with industry partners on issues at the frontier of organizational and managerial relevance. You will join a dynamic group of researchers working on governance, strategic change, and cross-sector collaboration. School of Business and Economics We at the School of Business and Economics (SBE) at VU Amsterdam bring together socially relevant teaching and research in the areas of business administration and economics. We focus on real-life issues that have a huge impact on society, economics and ecology: from robotics to big data, and from job market participation to change management. Collaboration and cooperation, transparency and social responsibility are four keywords that embody our approach. Students, researchers and staff at SBE share real-world knowledge in order to devise solutions together to global economic and social challenges. In order to make a positive impact on the world, society and the lives of others. Are you interested in joining SBE? You will work in a stimulating, dynamic and international environment with motivated colleagues dedicated to helping society make informed choices. SBE employs roughly 600 staff, with 7,250 students enrolled in the Bachelor’s and Master’s programmes and 1,700 executive students. Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam stands for values-driven education and research. We are open-minded experts with the ability to think freely - a broader mind. Maintaining an entrepreneurial perspective and concentrating on diversity, significance and humanity, we work on sustainable solutions with social impact. By joining forces, across the boundaries of disciplines, we work towards a better world for people and planet. Together we create a safe and respectful working and study climate, and an inspiring environment for education and research. Learn more about our codes of conduct We are located on one physical campus, in the heart of Amsterdam's Zuidas business district, with excellent location and accessibility.  Over 6,150 staff work at the VU and over 31,000 students attend academic education. Diversity Diversity is the driving force of VU Amsterdam. VU wants to be accessible and receptive to diversity in disciplines, cultures, ideas, nationalities, beliefs, preferences and worldviews. We believe that trust, respect, interest and differences lead to new insights and innovation, to sharpness and clarity, to excellence and a broader understanding. We stand for an inclusive community and believe that diversity and internationalisation contribute to the quality of education, research and our services. Therefore, we are always searching for people whose backgrounds and experience contribute to the diversity of the VU community. Vragen over de vacature? Neem contact op met dr. Ella Hafermalz Associate Professor Stuur een e-mail Interested Are you interested in this position and do you believe that your experience will contribute to the further development of our university? In that case, we encourage you to submit your application. For your application, we would like to learn something about how you think and what you notice. In a one page cover letter, please introduce yourself and your interest in the specific position briefly, and then address the following assignment: Drawing on your own experience of generative AI in a workplace, university or other institution, as well as some additional reading, tell us what you think “AI anomie” might mean. Ground your answer in something you have actually experienced or observed. There is no correct answer. We are interested in how you make sense of a specific experience and how you connect to our project’s topic. In a two page CV , please share relevant experience, including your masters programme and grades. Submitting a diploma and a reference check are part of the application process. Applications received by e-mail will not be considered. Acquisition in response to this advertisement is not appreciated. Vragen over de vacature? Neem contact op met dr. Ella Hafermalz Associate Professor Stuur een e-mail Sollicitatieprocedure Stap 1 Sollicitatie ontvangen Wij hebben je sollicitatie goed ontvangen. We streven ernaar om je zo snel mogelijk een eerste reactie te geven. Normaal gesproken is dat twee weken na het verstrijken van de deadline. Stap 2 Screening CV Een selectiecommissie beoordeelt of je voldoet aan de gestelde wensen/eisen voor de vacature. Bij een positieve beoordeling nodigen we je uit voor een eerste (hybride) sollicitatiegesprek. Stap 3 1e Gesprek Sollicitatiegesprekken vinden veelal plaats op een VU- locatie, maar soms word je eerst uitgenodigd voor een videogesprek. De VU maakt hiervoor gebruik van MS teams. Stap 4 2e Gesprek Voor het tweede gesprek vragen we je meestal om naar onze VU campus te komen. Stap 5 Pre-employment screening Het overleggen van een diploma wordt altijd gevraagd bij indiensttreding. Afhankelijk van de functie kan een assessment onderdeel uitmaken van de sollicitatieprocedure. Stap 6 Optioneel antecedentenonderzoek Afhankelijk van de functie en het bijbehorende risicoprofiel, kan een antecedentenonderzoek door de VU worden uitgevoerd. Daarbij kan worden gevraagd om referenties en/of een Verklaring omtrent Gedrag (VOG) aan te leveren. In het kader van kennisveiligheid kunnen wij ook publieke beschikbare social media accounts en publieke uitlatingen van jou op internet bekijken. Stap 7 In dienst Welkom je bent in dienst bij de Vrije Universiteit!! Andere interessante vacatures PhD kandidaat: De-escaleren van agressie tussen frontliniewerkers en burgers 1 / 1 Faculteit der Sociale en Geesteswetenschappen PhD WO / MSc Closes on 22-10-2026 Publicatiedatum 23-09-2026 0.0 km Hoe kunnen agenten, conducteurs en winkelmedewerkers agressie de-escaleren, en wat veroorzaakt deze agressie? Wil je hier onderzoek naar doen in samenwerking met maatschappelijke partners? Dan is dit misschien jouw baan. Bekijk vacature International Support Assistant 0,6 / 0,6 Student- & Onderwijszaken Overig ondersteunend HBO / Bachelor Closes on 11-10-2026 Publicatiedatum 23-09-2026 0.0 km Vind je het leuk om internationale studenten te begeleiden in he"
    },
    "https://werkenbij.vu.nl/vacatures/phd-position-in-communication-science-explaining-susceptibility-to-health-hacks-amsterdam-1343265": {
      "title": "PhD position in Communication Science: Explaining Susceptibility to Health Hacks",
      "description": "PhD position in Communication Science: Explaining Susceptibility to Health Hacks | Werken bij VU Vacatures Faculteit/Dienst Faculteiten Academisch Centrum Tandheelkunde Amsterdam (ACTA) Faculteit der Bètawetenschappen Faculteit der Gedrags- en Bewegingswetenschappen Faculteit der  Sociale en Geesteswetenschappen Faculteit der Geneeskunde Faculteit der Rechtsgeleerdheid School of Business and Economics Diensten Bestuurszaken Communicatie & Marketing Dienst Universitaire Relaties & Fondswerving Facilitaire Campus Organisatie Financiën HRM, Arbo & Milieu Informatietechnologie Student- en Onderwijszaken Universiteitsbibliotheek Jong talent PhD Student-assistent Stage Over ons Over ons VU arbeidsvoorwaarden: uitgebreid en flexibel VU persoonlijk: ontwikkelen, erkennen en waarderen VU identiteit: veilig, verantwoord en inclusief VU Campus: groen, rookvrij en sportief VU sollicitatie: proces, screening en participatie Ik ben een kandidaat Ga naar mijn persoonlijke pagina Log in als medewerker Log in 0 NL EN PhD position in Communication Science: Explaining Susceptibility to Health Hacks Ga terug VU Hoofdgebouw 1 / 1 MSc Publicatiedatum 02-10-2026 Amsterdam Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities Sluit op 26-10-2026 Solliciteer direct PhD position in Communication Science: Explaining Susceptibility to Health Hacks VU Hoofdgebouw Amsterdam 1 / 1 Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities MSc Sluit op 26-10-2026 Publicatiedatum 02-10-2026 Solliciteer direct Are you interested in why young people are drawn to quick health tips on social media, even when these tips may do more harm than good? Then this PhD project might be for you. Your function Social media are full of \"health hacks\": short, practical tips promising quick improvements in health, wellbeing, or appearance. While many are harmless, others are misleading or even hazardous, ranging from extreme detox regimens and homemade sunscreen recipes to unproven remedies promoted as alternatives to medical treatment. This PhD position is part of Hooked by the Health Hack, a four-year research project funded by NWO (Open Competition SSH) and carried out by Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam and Erasmus University Rotterdam. The project examines how young adults encounter and evaluate health hacks, and why some hacks are so persuasive, focusing on the interplay between individual characteristics, features of the online context, and cognitive processing during exposure. The project focuses on young adults aged 18–25. In this life stage of growing independence and identity exploration, health beliefs, routines, and self-perceptions are actively taking shape. This makes young adults a particularly relevant group for understanding both the appeal and the consequences of online health hacks. The project will generate knowledge to inform user-centred strategies that strengthen resilience to misleading and harmful health advice online. These strategies aim to support critical evaluation while respecting people's autonomy in making their own health decisions. The project brings together communication science, health psychology, and behavioural and cognitive science in an integrated mixed-methods design. Two closely aligned PhD tracks follow a shared three-stage research route: (1) identifying key individual and contextual factors, (2) examining the cognitive processes through which these factors shape evaluations during exposure, and (3) translating these insights into intervention strategies. Track A uses participatory research methods; Track B uses survey and experimental research. The two PhD candidates work closely together to develop a shared conceptual model and co-create interventions. This vacancy concerns Track B, based at the Communication Science department of Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam. Your duties Your project: Track B — Survey and experimental research Track B provides the explanatory, mechanism-testing perspective within the project: it examines how and why individual and contextual factors make people susceptible to hazardous health hacks, by testing the cognitive processes at work during actual exposure to these hacks. Your work draws on dual-process theories of reasoning, distinguishing fast, intuitive processing from slower, reflective evaluation. Your work will include: Conducting a multimodal content analysis of hazardous online health hacks. Designing and fielding a large-scale panel survey on individual and contextual susceptibility factors. Conducting controlled online and/or laboratory experiments on intuitive and reflective processing during exposure to health hacks. Experimentally evaluating intervention strategies co-developed with Track A. Integrating quantitative findings with Track A’s participatory research in a joint mixed-methods synthesis. Publishing research, presenting at conferences, and contributing to knowledge exchange with practice partners. Your profile A completed (research) Master's degree in Communication Science, (Media, Social, or Cognitive) Psychology, or a related social science discipline, obtained by the start of the position. Strong quantitative research skills, including survey design and experimental design. Proficiency in statistical analysis (e.g., in R) and, preferably, experience with advanced techniques such as multilevel modelling, mediation/moderation analysis, or structural equation modelling. Affinity with, or willingness to quickly develop expertise in, dual-process theories of reasoning and cognitive processing paradigms. Genuine interest in health communication, misinformation, and the psychology of persuasion. Excellent academic writing and communication skills in English. A collaborative mindset and willingness to work closely with a parallel PhD track with a different methodological orientation, and to contribute to genuinely interdisciplinary integration. The ability to work independently and take ownership of a substantial part of a multi-year, multi-stage research project. What do we offer? A full-time PhD position for a total of four years (initial contract of one year, extended after a positive assessment), embedded in the Department of Communication Science at Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam and its graduate school. Your dissertation at the end of the fourth year forms the end of your employment contract. A salary of € 3.204,00 (PhD) and maximum € 4.051,00 (PhD) gross per month in the fourth year, for a full-time employment. Close supervision, embedded in a well-resourced, interdisciplinary team. A genuinely collaborative research environment, with a sister PhD position (Track A) at Erasmus University Rotterdam and links to national networks in media literacy and digital wellbeing. We also offer you attractive fringe benefits and arrangements. Some examples: A full-time 38-hour working week comes with a holiday leave entitlement of 232 hours per year. If you choose to work 40 hours, you have 96 extra holiday leave hours on an annual basis. For part-timers, this is calculated pro rata. 8% holiday allowance and 8.3% end-of-year bonus contribution to commuting expenses optional model for designing a personalized benefits package a wide range of sports facilities which staff may use at a modest charge About us Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities A better understanding of ourselves and social communities, values and meaning, through social sciences and humanities. That is the aim of The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities. No single discipline can do this alone. The SSH disciplines are characterised by an open way of thinking and the natural urge to seek coherence and connection. The faculty consists of nine academic departments within three schools: School of Religion & Theology (SRT), School of Social Sciences (SSc) and School of Humanities (SH). Are you interested in joining Social Sciences and Humanities? You become part of a dynamic academic community with approximately 900 staff members, over 5,000 regular students and more than 5,000 course participants. In an inspiring and collegial environment, we work together on education and research with impact. Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam stands for values-driven education and research. We are open-minded experts with the ability to think freely - a broader mind. Maintaining an entrepreneurial perspective and concentrating on diversity, significance and humanity, we work on sustainable solutions with social impact. By joining forces, across the boundaries of disciplines, we work towards a better world for people and planet. Together we create a safe and respectful working and study climate, and an inspiring environment for education and research. Learn more about our codes of conduct We are located on one physical campus, in the heart of Amsterdam's Zuidas business district, with excellent location and accessibility. Over 6,150 staff work at the VU and over 31,000 students attend academic education. Diversity Diversity is the driving force of VU Amsterdam. VU wants to be accessible and receptive to diversity in disciplines, cultures, ideas, nationalities, beliefs, preferences and worldviews. We believe that trust, respect, interest and differences lead to new insights and innovation, to sharpness and clarity, to excellence and a broader understanding. We stand for an inclusive community and believe that diversity and internationalisation contribute to the quality of education, research and our services. Therefore, we are always searching for people whose backgrounds and experience contribute to the diversity of the VU community. Interested Are you interested? Please send your CV, a motivation letter (max. 1 page) explaining your interest in and fit with this position, and a relevant writing sample (e.g., thesis chapter or publication) by Monday October 26th (23:59 hr). Interviews (in Amsterdam or online) are expected to take place between 2-14 November. Expected start date is 1 January 2027. For questions about the position, please contact dr. M.A. Tanis, [email protected] . Applications received by e-mail will not be considered. Acquisition in response to this advertisement is not appreciated. Your function Social media are full of \"health hacks\": short, practical tips promising quick improvements in health, wellbeing, or appearance. While many are harmless, others are misleading or even hazardous, ranging from extreme detox regimens and homemade sunscreen recipes to unproven remedies promoted as alternatives to medical treatment. This PhD position is part of Hooked by the Health Hack, a four-year research project funded by NWO (Open Competition SSH) and carried out by Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam and Erasmus University Rotterdam. The project examines how young adults encounter and evaluate health hacks, and why some hacks are so persuasive, focusing on the interplay between individual characteristics, features of the online context, and cognitive processing during exposure. The project focuses on young adults aged 18–25. In this life stage of growing independence and identity exploration, health beliefs, routines, and self-perceptions are actively taking shape. This makes young adults a particularly relevant group for understanding both the appeal and the consequences of online health hacks. The project will generate knowledge to inform user-centred strategies that strengthen resilience to misleading and harmful health advice online. These strategies aim to support critical evaluation while respecting people's autonomy in making their own health decisions. The project brings together communication science, health psychology, and behavioural and cognitive science in an integrated mixed-methods design. Two closely aligned PhD tracks follow a shared three-stage research route: (1) identifying key individual and contextual factors, (2) examining the cognitive processes through which these factors shape evaluations during exposure, and (3) translating these insights into intervention strategies. Track A uses participatory research methods; Track B uses survey and experimental research. The two PhD candidates work closely together to develop a shared conceptual model and co-create interventions. This vacancy concerns Track B, based at the Communication Science department of Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam. Your duties Your project: Track B — Survey and experimental research Track B provides the explanatory, mechanism-testing perspective within the project: it examines how and why individual and contextual factors make people susceptible to hazardous health hacks, by testing the cognitive processes at work during actual exposure to these hacks. Your work draws on dual-process theories of reasoning, distinguishing fast, intuitive processing from slower, reflective evaluation. Your work will include: Conducting a multimodal content analysis of hazardous online health hacks. Designing and fielding a large-scale panel survey on individual and contextual susceptibility factors. Conducting controlled online and/or laboratory experiments on intuitive and reflective processing during exposure to health hacks. Experimentally evaluating intervention strategies co-developed with Track A. Integrating quantitative findings with Track A’s participatory research in a joint mixed-methods synthesis. Publishing research, presenting at conferences, and contributing to knowledge exchange with practice partners. Your profile A completed (research) Master's degree in Communication Science, (Media, Social, or Cognitive) Psychology, or a related social science discipline, obtained by the start of the position. Strong quantitative research skills, including survey design and experimental design. Proficiency in statistical analysis (e.g., in R) and, preferably, experience with advanced techniques such as multilevel modelling, mediation/moderation analysis, or structural equation modelling. Affinity with, or willingness to quickly develop expertise in, dual-process theories of reasoning and cognitive processing paradigms. Genuine interest in health communication, misinformation, and the psychology of persuasion. Excellent academic writing and communication skills in English. A collaborative mindset and willingness to work closely with a parallel PhD track with a different methodological orientation, and to contribute to genuinely interdisciplinary integration. The ability to work independently and take ownership of a substantial part of a multi-year, multi-stage research project. What do we offer? A full-time PhD position for a total of four years (initial contract of one year, extended after a positive assessment), embedded in the Department of Communication Science at Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam and its graduate school. Your dissertation at the end of the fourth year forms the end of your employment contract. A salary of € 3.204,00 (PhD) and maximum € 4.051,00 (PhD) gross per month in the fourth year, for a full-time employment. Close supervision, embedded in a well-resourced, interdisciplinary team. A genuinely collaborative research environment, with a sister PhD position (Track A) at Erasmus University Rotterdam and links to national networks in media literacy and digital wellbeing. We also offer you attractive fringe benefits and arrangements. Some examples: A full-time 38-hour working week comes with a holiday leave entitlement of 232 hours per year. If you choose to work 40 hours, you have 96 extra holiday leave hours on an annual basis. For part-timers, this is calculated pro rata. 8% holiday allowance and 8.3% end-of-year bonus contribution to commuting expenses optional model for designing a personalized benefits package a wide range of sports facilities which staff may use at a modest charge About us Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities A better understanding of ourselves and social communities, values and meaning, through social sciences and humanities. That is the aim of The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities. No single discipline can do this alone. The SSH disciplines are characterised by an open way of thinking and the natural urge to seek coherence and connection. The faculty consists of nine academic departments within three schools: School of Religion & Theology (SRT), School of Social Sciences (SSc) and School of Humanities (SH). Are you interested in joining Social Sciences and Humanities? You become part of a dynamic academic community with approximately 900 staff members, over 5,000 regular students and more than 5,000 course participants. In an inspiring and collegial environment, we work together on education and research with impact. Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam stands for values-driven education and research. We are open-minded experts with the ability to think freely - a broader mind. Maintaining an entrepreneurial perspective and concentrating on diversity, significance and humanity, we work on sustainable solutions with social impact. By joining forces, across the boundaries of disciplines, we work towards a better world for people and planet. Together we create a safe and respectful working and study climate, and an inspiring environment for education and research. Learn more about our codes of conduct We are located on one physical campus, in the heart of Amsterdam's Zuidas business district, with excellent location and accessibility. Over 6,150 staff work at the VU and over 31,000 students attend academic education. Diversity Diversity is the driving force of VU Amsterdam. VU wants to be accessible and receptive to diversity in disciplines, cultures, ideas, nationalities, beliefs, preferences and worldviews. We believe that trust, respect, interest and differences lead to new insights and innovation, to sharpness and clarity, to excellence and a broader understanding. We stand for an inclusive community and believe that diversity and internationalisation contribute to the quality of education, research and our services. Therefore, we are always searching for people whose backgrounds and experience contribute to the diversity of the VU community. Vragen over de vacature? Neem contact op met Dr. Martin Tanis Associate Professor 020 598 6854 (secretariat) Stuur een e-mail Interested Are you interested? Please send your CV, a motivation letter (max. 1 page) explaining your interest in and fit with this position, and a relevant writing sample (e.g., thesis chapter or publication) by Monday October 26th (23:59 hr). Interviews (in Amsterdam or online) are expected to take place between 2-14 November. Expected start date is 1 January 2027. For questions about the position, please contact dr. M.A. Tanis, [email protected] . Applications received by e-mail will not be considered. Acquisition in response to this advertisement is not appreciated. Vragen over de vacature? Neem contact op met Dr. Martin Tanis Associate Professor 020 598 6854 (secretariat) Stuur een e-mail Sollicitatieprocedure Andere interessante vacatures Directeur bedrijfsvoering faculteit der Bètawetenschappen 1 / 1 Faculteit der Bètawetenschappen Leidinggevende functie WO / MSc Sluit op 15-10-2026 Publicatiedatum 30-09-2026 0.0 km We zoeken een ervaren directeur bedrijfsvoering met een inhoudelijk bètatechnisch hart die verder bouwt aan een faculteit in beweging richting een solide en veerkrachtige organisatie. Bekijk vacature Junior Projectleider CDWS 0,6 / 0,6 Bestuurszaken Ontwikkeling & Innovatie WO / MSc Sluit op 11-10-2026 Publicatiedatum 01-10-2026 0.0 km Wil jij bijdragen aan de versterking van de weerbaarheid van onze samenleving? Ben jij een proactieve organisator met politiek-bestuurlijke sensitiviteit die moeiteloos schakelt tussen verschillende niveaus en stakeholders? Bekijk vacature Managementassistent Faculteit der Sociale en Geesteswetenschappen 0,8 / 0,8 Faculteit der Sociale en Geesteswetenschappen Overig ondersteunend MBO Sluit op 18-10-2026 Publicatiedatum 28-09-2026 0.0 km Ben jij iemand die energie krijgt van sa"
